    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n- [X] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version\n\n### Module(s)\n\nMini.ai\n\n### Description\n\nMini.ai updated expr_textobject fall back code, but it is wrong, let me explain this.\r\nFirst, if I use Colemak keymaps, paste below\r\n```\r\n-- colemak movement\r\nvim.keymap.set(\"\", \"e\", \"v:count == 0 ? 'gj' : 'j'\", { expr = true, silent = true })\r\nvim.keymap.set(\"\", \"u\", \"v:count == 0 ? 'gk' : 'k'\", { expr = true, silent = true })\r\nvim.keymap.set(\"\", \"n\", \"h\")\r\nvim.keymap.set(\"\", \"i\", \"l\")\r\n\r\n-- colemak jump to start/end of the line\r\nvim.keymap.set(\"\", \"N\", \"^\")\r\nvim.keymap.set(\"\", \"I\", \"$\")\r\n-- colemak fast navigation\r\nvim.keymap.set(\"\", \"U\", \"5k\")\r\nvim.keymap.set(\"\", \"E\", \"5j\")\r\n\r\n-- colemak insert key\r\nvim.keymap.set(\"\", \"k\", \"i\")\r\nvim.keymap.set(\"\", \"K\", \"I\")\r\nvim.keymap.set(\"\", \"gk\", \"gi\", { desc = \"goto last insert\" })\r\n\r\n-- colemake undo key\r\nvim.keymap.set(\"\", \"l\", \"u\")\r\nvim.keymap.set(\"\", \"L\", \"U\")\r\n\r\n-- colemak end of word\r\nvim.keymap.set(\"\", \"h\", \"e\")\r\nvim.keymap.set(\"\", \"H\", \"K\")\r\n\r\n-- colemake searching key\r\n-- https://github.com/mhinz/vim-galore#saner-behavior-of-n-and-n\r\nvim.keymap.set(\"\", \"=\", \"'Nn'[v:searchforward]\", { expr = true })\r\nvim.keymap.set(\"\", \"-\", \"'nN'[v:searchforward]\", { expr = true })\r\n```\r\nI map `i` to `l` and `k` to `i`. So when setup mini.ai mappings below\r\n```\r\naround = \"a\",\r\ninside = \"k\",\r\n```\r\nSo when I typed `ckw`, it's a vim built-in command, so mini.ai will fall back, return string \"kw\",  but `k`  is not treated as `i`, because mini.ai already overwritten the keymap of `k`,  'k'  is a  native k here to avoid infinite recursive. \r\nbut when changed code like this\r\n```\r\nif H.is_disabled() or not H.is_valid_textobject_id(tobj_id) then\r\n\tlocal res = ai_type .. tobj_id\r\n\r\n\tif vim.fn.maparg(res, mode) ~= \"\" then\r\n\t\tres = H.keys.ignore .. res\r\n\tend\r\n\treturn res\r\nend\r\n```\r\nIt's still not what I expected, because `i` was map to `l` and `i` hasn't recursive problem, so nvim will respect user custom keymap.\r\nFinally I changed code to making it work\r\n```\r\nif H.is_disabled() or not H.is_valid_textobject_id(tobj_id) then\r\n\tlocal res = ai_type .. tobj_id\r\n\tlocal ai_mapping = vim.fn.maparg(ai_type, mode)\r\n\tif ai_mapping ~= \"\" then\r\n\t\tvim.keymap.del(mode, ai_type)\r\n\t\tvim.defer_fn(function()\r\n\t\t\t\tvim.keymap.set(mode, ai_type, ai_mapping)\r\n\t\tend, 100)\r\n\tend\r\n\r\n\tif vim.fn.maparg(res, mode) ~= \"\" then\r\n\t\tres = H.keys.ignore .. res\r\n\tend\r\n\treturn res\r\nend\r\n```\r\n\r\nIt's ugly, but works.\r\nForgive my poor English, I hope I explained clearly\n\n### Neovim version\n\n0.8.2\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nFollow my  keymaps\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nCompatible with user-defined keys\n\n### Actual behavior\n\nNot work as my expected",
